course: dictionary
lesson: 6037
title: Melting: solid to liquid
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Melting means changing from a solid into a liquid because it gets warm. Ice turning into water is the easiest example to picture.
explain: «Melting is a solid turning into a liquid.» Look for the clues: something solid, heat, and then something runny. Melting can also describe something becoming soft, like «melting chocolate».
explain: Scientists call the temperature where a solid melts its melting point. Ice melts at 0 °C (32 °F). Melting can be undone: water freezes back into ice when it cools.
explain: In stories, melting can mean becoming softer in feeling, such as a heart melting with kindness. The context tells you which meaning the author means.
fact: Melting means a solid turns into a liquid when it gets warm.
fact: Ice melts at 0 °C (32 °F), and this temperature is its melting point.
fact: Chocolate melts at about 34 °C and can set again, so melting is a change that can be undone.
fact: Melting is a reversible change because the molecules stay the same. Freezing is the reverse of melting.
fact: In geography, melting glaciers and ice sheets raise sea levels.
exercise: «Butter in the pan slowly turned into a yellow liquid.» What is happening to the butter? | Freezing | *Melting | Growing | Shrinking
exercise: Ice melts at 0 °C. What is that temperature called? | *Its melting point | Its size | Its weight | Its boiling point
exercise: Which change can be undone? | *Melting chocolate that sets again | Toasting bread | Frying an egg | Baking a cake
exercise: «The ice cubes began melting in the sun.» Find and copy one word that means turning from a solid into a liquid. | sun | *melting | ice | cubes
test: In science, what is a melting point? | *The temperature at which a solid turns into a liquid | The temperature at which a liquid turns into a gas | The coldest temperature in the fridge | The weight of a melted block
test: Which change is the reverse of melting? | Dissolving | *Freezing | Burning | Boiling
exam: Which change cannot be undone, so it is not melting? | Boiling water | *Frying an egg | Freezing water | Melting butter
